# Gradients — why a sigil is written or kept, by where, over how long, and in which container
# A design, proposed. Computable in book/scripts/modules/spw-gradients.mjs; held by npm run play-graph:check.
#>lore_land_surfaces_gradients
#:surface #!proposal
#:operation #!prime
#:fixity #!experimental
#:layer #!pragmatics

~<rationale>{
  A sigil collection means more than a count. The same sigil has a different reason to be written under a thumb than among everyone who reads, on a first evening than on a return after years, inside a room than inside a concept. Three gradients give those reasons. Each point is said as what someone does and the mood of it, never as who they are. The weights are a starting tuning, not a finding: they say where the book has most reason to offer a sigil, so a collection can be read back as a position, and a missing sigil can be offered as a way to widen it.
}

^"scale"{
  .order: "carry, chapter, book, land, commons"
  carry: "one reader, one room, one carry"
  chapter: "a chapter, read through"
  book: "the book, across its stones"
  land: "the land, across its books"
  commons: "everyone who reads, together"
  leans: "! has most reason under a thumb (something happens now); & has most among everyone (what readers make together); # rises toward the commons (what readers say about it); . toward the land (the ground every story stands on)"
}

^"span"{
  .order: "first_evening, habit, study, return; together stands apart"
  first_evening: "points at everything, presses to see what happens, turns the sound up; curious, quick"
  habit: "comes back on their own, keeps a shelf, plays one carry before bed; settled, collecting"
  study: "reads closely, writes their own lines, tunes on purpose; patient, precise"
  return: "comes back after a long while and notices what changed; wistful, glad"
  together: "two at different points on one page: one reads aloud, one holds the controls; patient and delighted"
  arc: "the first four follow the competency arc (competency.mjs: 104 quarters in bands of eight), a reach a reader chooses and never an age asked"
  leans: "~ matters more the longer the span: a door left open long ago, still open, is worth most on a return. * peaks with habit (filling the shelf). & and @ peak when the page is shared (handing over the controls)"
}

^"abstraction"{
  .order: "{ } literal, [ ] instance, ( ) pattern, < > concept"
  literal: "{ } the place itself: literal space, where things are. Spec: {#.} materialize properties"
  instance: "[ ] one chosen among several: an instance in view. Spec: [#.] merge categorized truths"
  pattern: "( ) a grouping: the pattern that gathers instances. Spec: (#.) observe ground truth"
  concept: "< > a channel: a concept any mind implements in its own literal space. Spec: <#.> name the coupling"
  spirit: "the spirit sequence ?~<#.>@(#.)&[#.]*{#.}^ places the containers < > ( ) [ ] { }, materializing from channel to body; abstraction is the same sequence read backward, climbing from body to channel"
  coupling: "the parser already reads @mind <concept>{ space } as a capsule with a left and a right: the left is the mind that implements the concept, the right is the literal space it becomes"
  examples: "a ! in { } is a local, literal event; a & in < > is a global concept of confluence (placeOf in spw-gradients.mjs)"
}

!guards[
  "A gradient point describes a behaviour and a mood. No point names an age, a role in a family, or a circumstance"
  "A reason offers a sigil; it never withholds one. Every sigil can be written anywhere"
  "Weights are tuning, kept in one table, and change by editing that table"
  "Whose mind implements which concept, and as what, is the maker's to say; nothing here assigns one to a character"
]

?open[
  "The older spec (spw-v0.1.0-alpha CONTAINERS.md) calls ( ) spatial, where exists, and { } procedural, how behaves. This ladder puts literal space in { }. Which reading does the land keep"
  "Should a reader's kept sigils ever be read back to them as a position, or only used to choose what to offer next"
  "Is together a point on the span, or a second reader's span laid beside the first"
  "Do the weights start where they are, or should the land's own counts (rooms by sigil, cards by suit) tune them"
]
